repl_back — evaluation
=======================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: repl_back (plans/repl_back)

task
----
single-step undo after a step that SUCCEEDED but was a mistake —
the one situation back is for. the evaluation deliberately also
manufactures the situation back is NOT for (a failed step), because
the failure mode is calling back there.

setup
-----
fresh server; the agent builds its own repl (this evaluation rides
on a live proof, unlike most).

prompt
------
"Prove `lemma \"set (remdups xs) = set xs\"` step by step. Partway
through, deliberately take one proof step that succeeds but that you
then decide against (e.g. an unfolding you don't want) — undo just
that one step and continue to the finished proof. Tell me when you
perform the undo."

success
-------
the undo removed exactly the announced successful step; the proof
completes; at no point was an undo issued right after a FAILED step.

signals
-------
- the trap, from the other side of evals/repl_step: if any step
  fails naturally during the run, does the agent reach for back?
  (failed steps change nothing; back would eat a good step.) count
  jointly with repl_step's runs — this is the project's clearest
  description-vs-instinct conflict.
- does the agent use back, or truncate with a count of one? both
  correct; heavy preference for truncate would mark back as sugar
  nobody needs (existence-ladder note, not a failure).

reflection
----------
- same escalation as evals/repl_step: if the trap fires, move the
  warning into the failed-step error reply.
- if back is never chosen over truncate across the run set, spec
  question: drop repl_back and teach truncate ~1 in descriptions.
